GETENV(3)                      P32W Unix                       GETENV(3)

NAME
       getenv, setenv, unsetenv, environ - the environment

SYNOPSIS
       #include <stdlib.h>

       extern char **environ;
       char *getenv(const char *name);
       int setenv(const char *name, const char *value, int overwrite);
       int unsetenv(const char *name);

DESCRIPTION
       The environment is a list of strings of the form name=value,
       passed from a program to the programs it runs. crt0 sets
       environ to the list given to main as its third argument (a
       NULL ends it). login(1) starts the shell with PATH, HOME,
       USER and SHELL, and sh(1) passes on all its variables (TZ,
       LINES, COLUMNS, ...).

       getenv returns the value of the variable name: the text after
       the = of the first string that starts name=.

       setenv sets name to value: it makes a new string name=value
       in memory from malloc(3) and puts it in place of the old one,
       or, for a new name, at the end of the list (copied to the
       heap the first time it grows). If name is set already and
       overwrite is 0, nothing changes.

       unsetenv takes every string for name out of the list.

       execv, execvp, execl and execlp (exec(2)) pass environ to the
       new program; execve passes the list it is given.

RETURN VALUE
       getenv returns the value, or NULL if name is not set. Do not
       change the string it returns. setenv returns 0, or -1 if
       there was not enough memory. unsetenv returns 0.

EXAMPLES
       char *home = getenv("HOME");
       if (home == NULL)
           home = "/";
       setenv("TZ", "PST8PDT", 1);
       tzset();

SEE ALSO
       sh(1), exec(2), tzset(3)

BUGS
       setenv does not check name for an = or for being empty. The
       strings replaced are never freed. There is no putenv or
       clearenv.
